## 12. Key risks and safeguards

| Risk | Safeguard |
|---|---|
| Polite language is mistaken for agreement | Confirm decisions in writing and ask for operational restatement |
| Negotiators lack authority | Map formal and informal decision-makers early |
| Cultural stereotypes distort judgment | Validate assumptions through observation and direct questions |
| Relationship pressure weakens diligence | Keep objective approval gates and independent verification |
| Translation changes legal meaning | Use professional translators and define the controlling language |
| One side makes repeated unilateral concessions | Use conditional, reciprocal package trades |
| Contributions are vague or overstated | Define measurable deliverables, valuation, and milestones |
| IP or data is misused | Limit access, use licensing, and establish technical controls and audit rights |
| Governance deadlock paralyzes the venture | Establish escalation, mediation, and workable exit mechanisms |
| Local conduct creates compliance exposure | Apply parent-company standards, training, monitoring, and audit rights |
| Key sponsor leaves | Institutionalize relationships and document obligations |
| Parties agree strategically but cannot operate together | Use pilots and negotiate the operating model before closing |

## Recommended negotiation sequence

1. Complete internal objectives, BATNA, authority, and red-line analysis;
2. Conduct partner, country, legal, tax, and compliance research;
3. Form and culturally brief the negotiating team;
4. Hold relationship-building and strategic-alignment meetings;
5. Agree on a negotiation and communication protocol;
6. Sign an appropriate confidentiality agreement;
7. Map the partner’s decision-makers and approval process;
8. Exchange a high-level concept paper;
9. Negotiate a term sheet as a package;
10. Conduct due diligence and test the joint operating model;
11. Resolve governance, IP, funding, deadlock, and exit terms;
12. Obtain internal and regulatory approvals;
13. Execute definitive agreements and the 100-day launch plan;
14. Review the relationship and performance at scheduled intervals.

## Bottom line

The most effective cross-border negotiation strategy combines **cultural flexibility with commercial and legal discipline**. The company should adapt how it communicates, builds trust, and sequences discussions without weakening its standards for due diligence, compliance, governance, or value creation.

A successful agreement should demonstrate not only that the parties want the same commercial outcome, but also that they can make decisions, exchange information, resolve conflict, protect assets, and operate a shared company together over time.
